---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { getGitHubEditUrl, getGitHubRepositoryUrl } from "../lib/github";

const editUrl = getGitHubEditUrl("src/pages/how-it-works.astro");
const repositoryUrl = getGitHubRepositoryUrl();
---

<Layout title="How it works" description="Explore the architecture, content model, GitHub integration, and deployment pipeline behind Astro Pages Publisher.">
  <article class="docs-page editable-block">
    <p class="eyebrow">Inside the publisher</p>
    <h1>How it works</h1>
    <p class="docs-lead">
      Astro Pages Publisher keeps the website content, GitHub identity, reusable templates,
      and deployment process separate. That is what lets the same repository work after a fork
      or rename without editing account-specific values throughout the source.
    </p>

    <div class="feature-grid">
      <section class="feature-card">
        <h2>Content is editable</h2>
        <p>Site branding lives in JSON, long-form articles use Markdown, and pages are regular Astro files.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="feature-card">
        <h2>Templates are reusable</h2>
        <p>Starter files have one canonical copy in <code>templates/</code> and clean hidden working copies.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="feature-card">
        <h2>GitHub context is dynamic</h2>
        <p>The current owner, repository, branch, and Pages base path come from the build environment.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="feature-card">
        <h2>Deployments are verified</h2>
        <p>Checks and tests run before a static artifact receives GitHub Pages deployment permissions.</p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>1. Separate project identity from site branding</h2>
      <p>
        The repository identity is read from GitHub Actions context and validated centrally in
        <code>src/lib/site-env.mjs</code>. It drives GitHub links, source-edit links, the canonical URL,
        and the base path used by project sites. The visible brand, description, introduction,
        and footer text are independently configured in <code>src/content/site.json</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        A normal repository is published below <code>/REPOSITORY/</code>. A repository named
        <code>OWNER.github.io</code> is published at the domain root. A custom HTTPS domain can be
        configured with <code>SITE_URL</code> and <code>SITE_BASE</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>2. Build the site from content and templates</h2>
      <p>
        Articles in <code>src/content/blog/</code> are Markdown files with title and publication-date
        frontmatter. Pages in <code>src/pages/</code> become routes. The navigation generator discovers
        regular page files and footer pages, while ignoring hidden starter files and underscore-prefixed files.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <a href="../templates/">Templates guide</a> demonstrates the Markdown post, standard page,
        footer page, feature-page, and resource-collection starters. Rename a hidden starter before changing its contents;
        the workflow restores a clean copy from <code>templates/</code> automatically.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>3. Load repository data at build time</h2>
      <p>
        <code>src/lib/github.ts</code> is the single GitHub API integration. It loads the current account
        profile, public repositories, and recent commits during static generation. Requests use the
        public API without a personal access token; responses are cached within each build and failures
        are rendered as explicit status messages rather than silently shown as empty data.
      </p>
      <p>
        Explore the <a href="../repositories/">public repository list</a> and <a href="../commits/">recent commit feed</a>
        to see how the site reflects the GitHub account associated with the current repository.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>4. Validate and deploy with GitHub Actions</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Prepare or restore renamed starter files from the canonical templates.</li>
        <li>Generate navigation and check the editorial content state.</li>
        <li>Install the locked dependencies, run Astro validation, and execute the test suite.</li>
        <li>Build the static site and scan the output for leaked secrets and unsafe markup.</li>
        <li>Upload the verified <code>dist/</code> artifact, then deploy that exact artifact to GitHub Pages.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The preparation job has only the repository write permission needed to restore starter files.
        The build job is read-only, and the final deployment job receives the Pages deployment permissions.
      </p>
      <p>
        The workflow follows the repository's actual default branch. It does not require the branch to be
        named <code>main</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>Try the project</h2>
      <p>
        <a href={repositoryUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open the source repository</a>, fork it,
        rename the fork if needed, enable GitHub Pages with GitHub Actions, and personalize
        <code>src/content/site.json</code> plus the example posts.
      </p>
      <p><a href="../getting-started/">Follow the setup guide →</a></p>
    </section>

    <a href={editUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="edit-pencil" title="Edit this page">✎</a>
  </article>
</Layout>
